read-string-does-not-skip-comments — real AutoCAD/BricsCAD readers are stricter about comments than clautolisp's; line comments are --bricscad-specific, block comments break BOTH vendors
==========================================================================================================================================================================================

Status: FIX IMPLEMENTED in clautolisp 2.1.1 (2026-09-08) — root cause
fully characterized across both vendors and platforms; the per-dialect
behavior + advisory-warning design below is landed (BUILTIN-READ in
`clautolisp/autolisp-builtins-core/source/api.lisp`, the
`[read-leading-comment]` dialect-warning register entry in
`clautolisp/autolisp-runtime/source/portability.lisp`, and a new
per-dialect matrix test, `builtin-read-leading-comment-dialect-matrix`
in `clautolisp/autolisp-builtins-core/tests/builtin-tests.lisp`).
Remaining: the autolisp-spec `READ` page (autolisp-spec/documentation/
autolisp-visual-lisp-specification-draft.org) still needs the
Tested-Behaviour / dialect-divergence note this issue's original "To
check" item asked for — tracked as the next step, not yet done as of
this revision. Found while building an unrelated project
(autocad-gui-prototype, loader.lsp), which has worked around it
downstream by not calling `read` on a string at all (see Downstream
fix below) — that workaround stays in place regardless of the
clautolisp-side fix landing, since it predates it.

Summary
-------

  (read ";; foo\nbar")            -- LINE comment before the object

    BricsCAD V26 (real, macOS)    -> NIL
    BricsCAD V25 (real, Windows)  -> NIL   (same behaviour, x-platform)
    AutoCAD 2022 (real, Windows)  -> BAR   (skips it, like load)
    clautolisp (any dialect)     -> BAR

  (read ";|foo|; bar")            -- BLOCK comment before the object

    BricsCAD (V25/V26, both)     -> NIL
    AutoCAD 2022 (real, Windows)  -> NIL   (does NOT skip a block comment)
    clautolisp (any dialect)     -> BAR

So this is TWO separate findings, not one:

  - Line comments (`;` to end of line): a genuine, pure `--dialect
    bricscad` divergence. AutoCAD's reader matches clautolisp's own
    (lenient) behaviour here; only BricsCAD returns NIL.
  - Block comments (`;|...|;`): NOT bricscad-specific — BOTH real
    vendors tested return NIL, and only clautolisp is lenient. This is
    clautolisp being too permissive relative to EVERY real product
    tested, not a --bricscad-only gap.

In both cases the failure mode is the same shape: the reader returns
NIL the instant a comment (of either kind) is the first non-blank
thing it meets, before any real object — leading whitespace/newlines
before the comment or the object don't matter either way. Comments
trailing AFTER a first object, or interspersed between multiple
objects in one string across repeated `read` calls, are NOT
characterized here — narrower, separate question if it ever matters
to a caller.

Original repro (autocad-gui-prototype/loader.lsp): a small loader read
a metadata file (a foo.alpm, itself starting with four `;;;;` header-
comment lines, then a real `(alpm-define-system ...)` form) via
read-line + strcat (reconstructing the whole file into one string) and
a single `(read whole-string)` on the result. Under clautolisp this
returned the expected form every time headless — 130/130 tests green,
including an end-to-end reload of loader.lsp. On real BricsCAD V26 it
silently returned NIL, every single time, for all three files affected
(each starting with the same four-line comment header) — plain
`load`-based file loads elsewhere in the exact same project were
completely unaffected (they never call `read` on a string, only `load`
on a file). Confirmed via a live BricsCAD probe: the reconstructed
string was verified byte-correct (exact length, exact content,
comments included) via `strlen`/`princ`, then `(read that-string)`
still came back NIL. Root cause pinned down only after several other
hypotheses (SECURELOAD/TRUSTEDPATHS, boundp reliability on `C:`
symbols, literal-path identity, `foreach` vs. plain function nesting,
file encoding) were each tested live on BricsCAD and ruled out first.

Fix
---

pjb's 2026-09-08 ruling settled this as ONE mechanism covering both
comment kinds, not two independent tracks: `read` (string form)
consults the ACTIVE DIALECT for what to do when the first non-blank
content of its string argument is a comment, of either kind, before
any real object. Two axes — BEHAVIOR (what `read` returns) and WARN-P
(whether it also emits an advisory) — cross into this matrix:

  | Dialect              | leading `;` LINE        | leading `;|...|;` BLOCK   |
  |----------------------+--------------------------+---------------------------|
  | --dialect bricscad   | NIL, warns               | NIL, warns                |
  | --dialect autocad    | skip+return, silent      | NIL, warns                |
  | --strict             | skip+return, WARNS       | skip+return, WARNS        |
  | --dialect clautolisp | skip+return, silent      | skip+return, silent       |
  | --lax                | skip+return, silent      | skip+return, silent       |

Rationale for each row:

  - `--dialect bricscad`: reproduces real BricsCAD (V25 Windows / V26
    macOS) exactly for both comment kinds — NIL, every time, the
    instant the first thing `read` meets is a comment. Warns, because
    the caller is about to silently lose the object a clautolisp-only
    run would have handed back.
  - `--dialect autocad`: reproduces real AutoCAD 2022 (Windows)
    exactly — a leading LINE comment is skipped, matching clautolisp's
    own default, so that case is silent; a leading BLOCK comment
    returns NIL, so that case warns (the one place --autocad's own
    return value now diverges from clautolisp's default).
  - `--strict`: KEEPS clautolisp's own lenient skip-and-return
    behaviour for BOTH kinds (strict is not literally impersonating
    either vendor's engine) but WARNS for both, because strict means
    "portable across the real hosts I know about", and at least one
    real host disagrees for either comment kind — this is a genuine
    portability hazard even though nothing breaks under --strict
    itself.
  - `--dialect clautolisp` / `--lax`: unchanged, fully silent, for
    both kinds — the same deliberate, documented convenience already
    granted to every other clautolisp-only leniency (--lax's general
    "accept every vendor's extension without complaining" contract;
    --clautolisp's own blessed defaults).

Implemented as the `[read-leading-comment]` dialect-portability
warning (autolisp-spec ch.25 mechanism — the SAME advisory-warning
machinery `path-dotdot` / `ext-bricscad-undocumented` / etc. use, NOT
deferred-clautolisp-out-of-dialect-warnings.issue's still-unbuilt
MISSING-operator mechanism, which would not fire here anyway since
READ exists in every dialect):

  - Register entry (message template, per-dialect prose, references):
    `clautolisp/autolisp-runtime/source/portability.lisp`, tag
    `"read-leading-comment"`, kind `:vendor-divergence`.
  - Emitter + policy + the BUILTIN-READ rewrite:
    `clautolisp/autolisp-builtins-core/source/api.lisp` —
    `%READ-STRING-LEADING-COMMENT-KIND` (peeks the first token via the
    reader's own tokenizer, `:retain-comments-p t`, without disturbing
    the real parse), `%READ-LEADING-COMMENT-POLICY` (the matrix above,
    keyed off `AUTOLISP-DIALECT-PRODUCT` so it covers every BricsCAD /
    AutoCAD version+platform variant, not just `-v26` / `-2026`), and
    `EMIT-READ-LEADING-COMMENT-WARNING`. Not deduplicated — unlike a
    DEFUN/LAMBDA lambda-list, a READ call has no source-position
    "occurrence" to key a once-per-occurrence dedup on, so it fires
    every call, like `EMIT-ENTMAKE-MARKER-DIVERGENCE-WARNING` does for
    the same reason.
  - Test: `builtin-read-leading-comment-dialect-matrix` in
    `clautolisp/autolisp-builtins-core/tests/builtin-tests.lisp` —
    exercises all five matrix rows above end-to-end through the READ
    builtin, both comment kinds, asserting both the return value and
    the presence/absence of the `[read-leading-comment]` warning text
    on *ERROR-OUTPUT*.
  - Annex regenerated: `documentation/clautolisp-dialect-warnings.org`
    (via `make dialect-warnings-annex`) now documents the tag.
  - Version bump: DEVELOP 2.1.0 -> 2.1.1 in
    `clautolisp/tools/clautolisp/source/version.lisp`
    (clautolisp-version-bump convention).
  - NOT changed: what `read` does with a TRAILING comment (after the
    first object, or between repeated `read` calls on one string) —
    still out of scope per the original "To check" item 3 above; only
    the LEADING-comment case is characterized and fixed here.
  - Still open: the autolisp-spec `READ` function-entry page needs the
    Tested-Behaviour / per-dialect note this issue's original "To
    check" item 1 asked for (see Status above).

Downstream fix (autocad-gui-prototype, not clautolisp itself)
---------------------------------------------------------------

Two iterations, worth recording since the second one is the actual
lesson: a first pass (commit bfc057d) hand-rolled a character-by-character,
string-literal-aware comment stripper (both line and block forms) to
run before `read`. Working, but reimplementing a chunk of a Lisp
reader's tokenizer downstream is exactly the kind of thing worth
avoiding if there's a better option. There was: `alpm-define-system`
(~/src/ogamita/alpm/sources/alpm.lsp) is a real function, invoked by
ordinary evaluation when a .alpm file is `load`ed — the .alpm's own
top-level form is a function call, not inert data, once that name is
bound. Final fix (commit 309c5f2): a minimal local stub for
`alpm-define-system` (captures its `def` argument, none of the real
system's validation/registry machinery — no dependency on the real
ALPM package) installed before `load`ing the .alpm file directly.
`load` has never shown either comment-handling gap on any product
tested, so this sidesteps both findings above entirely rather than
trying to re-derive the exact vendor rules downstream. See
loader.lsp's own `fs-loader--alpm-plist`.

Found by
--------

Original macOS/BricsCAD V26 repro and root-cause isolation:
autocad-gui-prototype/loader.lsp live-BricsCAD debugging session,
2026-09-08 (Claude Sonnet 5, with pjb driving BricsCAD interactively).
Cross-platform/cross-vendor confirmation for BOTH the line-comment case
(BricsCAD V25 Windows matches V26 macOS; AutoCAD 2022 Windows matches
clautolisp instead) and the block-comment case (AutoCAD 2022 also
returns NIL, unlike its own line-comment behaviour) supplied directly
by pjb the same day, from separate own testing/knowledge — not
re-derived via a live probe in this session.

Related: deferred-clautolisp-out-of-dialect-warnings.issue (the
general per-dialect-behavior warning mechanism, discussed above as a
partial alternative/fallback to the direct fixes).
